1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Welcome back. Access control is where a lot of security really lives, and it's easy to get half of

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:07,000
it right and miss the other half.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Before your agent takes a privileged action, you have to prove who's calling and decide what that

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:15,000
caller is allowed to do.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:24,000
This lesson covers validating identity, approving high-stakes actions, and monitoring authorized access. Let's dig in.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:31,000
Let's start with the three ideas everything else builds on. The first is authentication. That's

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:32,000
verifying who a caller is.

8
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:39,000
The identity gets proven through a login, a token, or a key before anything else happens.

9
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:45,000
The second is authorization. That's deciding what a verified identity is actually allowed to do.

10
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Checking each requested action against that identity's permissions. Authentication asks who,

11
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,000
authorization asks what, and you need both.

12
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:01,000
The third is access approval. For sensitive operations, you require explicit sign-off, so a person

13
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:04,000
confirms a high-stakes action before it runs.

14
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Keep these three straight, because most access mistakes come from treating one of them as if it

15
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:12,000
covered the others. It doesn't.

16
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:19,000
Let's slow down on the distinction between authentication and authorization, because conflating them

17
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:20,000
is the single most common gap.

18
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Authentication, sometimes shortened to Authn, verifies who a caller is. The agent proves its

19
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,000
identity with a credential before the request goes any further.

20
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:36,000
That's it. That step answers identity and nothing about permission.

21
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Authorization, or Auth-Z, is the second question. It determines what that verified identity is

22
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,000
permitted to do. An agent needs both.

23
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,000
First, it proves identity, then it checks that the identity may perform the specific action being requested.

24
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:57,000
If you only do the first, you know who is calling, but you have said nothing about whether they're

25
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,000
allowed to do the thing they're asking for.

26
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,000
That's where trouble starts.

27
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Here's the trap that follows directly from that. A valid login isn't access. Being authenticated

28
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:12,000
doesn't grant permission, and developers assume it does all the time.

29
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Someone signs in successfully, and the code treats that as a green light for whatever comes next. It isn't.

30
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:26,000
A valid login proves identity, not entitlement to any particular action.

31
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:32,000
So you enforce per-action authorization on every sensitive call, checking each time whether this

32
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,000
identity may do this specific thing,

33
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:41,000
and do that check at the downstream system, the thing actually holding the data, or the capability,

34
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:43,000
rather than trusting the model to police itself.

35
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:51,000
The model can be talked into asking for something. The downstream authorization check is what refuses to serve it.

36
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:57,000
Some actions are risky enough that no amount of automated checking should be the last word.

37
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:03,000
For those, put a human in the loop. A person confirms the high-impact operation before it runs.

38
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Think about actions that delete data, move money, or change who has access.

39
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:13,000
You want the flow to gate the action first, get approval, and only then execute.

40
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:18,000
A common way to do this is escalating the sensitive call to a permission dialog.

41
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:22,000
So instead of proceeding, the agent pauses and asks a person to decide.

42
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:27,000
The key idea is that approval is enforcement, and it happens before the action.

43
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:32,000
It isn't a note you write down afterward. If the sign-off comes after the fact, it wasn't approval,

44
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:33,000
it was just a record.

45
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,000
Let's put the sequence together, because order matters here.

46
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Three steps run before a sensitive action executes.

47
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:48,000
First, validate identity. Confirm the caller with a proven credential so you actually know who is behind the request.

48
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:55,000
Second, authorize the action. Check that this specific identity is allowed to perform this specific operation,

49
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:59,000
scoped down to least privilege so it can reach only what it needs.

50
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:05,000
Third, approve when warranted. For the high-stakes operations, route the request to a person for

51
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:07,000
explicit sign-off before anything happens.

52
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Validate, authorize, approve. Each step is a separate gate, and each one can stop the action on its own.

53
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Skip any of them and you've left a door open that the other two can't close for you.

54
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:26,000
Let's look at where identity gets managed in practice, using three controls.

55
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:33,000
The first is single sign-on. You centralize login so identities are verified and managed in one place,

56
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:41,000
with domain capture and managed policy on the enterprise plan. Instead of scattered logins, nobody can audit.

57
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:48,000
The second is role-based permissions. Each identity gets a role that grants only the capabilities it needs.

58
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:56,000
A narrow role might be able to create only a narrow kind of key and nothing broader, while a fuller role can do more.

59
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:02,000
That's least privilege living at the identity layer. And least privilege is the third control itself.

60
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Grant an identity only the minimum its function needs, and remove everything unneeded.

61
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:13,000
So if it's ever hijacked, it inherits very little to abuse.

62
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:18,000
Now let's be precise about what monitoring does, because this is where people fool themselves.

63
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:24,000
Monitoring is detection, not control. Logging who accessed what tells you about misuse.

64
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:27,000
It doesn't stop misuse. That distinction is everything.

65
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Monitoring is a detection layer that sits on top of enforcement, not in place of it.

66
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:38,000
Passive logging records that an action happened, it never blocks the action from happening.

67
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:43,000
So if the only thing standing between an agent and a dangerous operation is that you'll see it in a

68
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,000
log later, you have no control at all.

69
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:52,000
You have a recording of the damage. Identity and authorization are what gate the action first.

70
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:58,000
Think of monitoring as the alarm, and authorization as the lock on the door.

71
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:02,000
So what is monitoring good for? Building the audit trail.

72
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:10,000
Continuous logging turns raw access into a compliance record and an anomaly signal, both valuable.

73
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:18,000
You record who accessed what and when, so operations can be reviewed and you can answer questions after an incident.

74
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:24,000
Beyond that, you watch authorized access for anomalies, patterns that hint at abuse, even when each

75
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:26,000
action looked permitted on its own.

76
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:31,000
One clarification keeps the categories clean.

77
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:37,000
A gate that blocks a configuration edit mid-session is enforcement, not monitoring.

78
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:43,000
Monitoring records and alerts, and the moment something blocks, it has crossed into enforcement.

79
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:48,000
But here's the part teams forget. A log only helps if someone reads it.

80
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:54,000
Review the trail regularly, because an audit log nobody looks at catches exactly nothing, no matter

81
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:55,000
how complete it is.

82
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:05,000
Access control is a sequence. Prove identity, authorize each action, approve the risky ones, and then monitor.

83
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:11,000
Authentication answers who, authorization answers what they can do, and you need both.

84
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:18,000
A valid login is not access. And remember, monitoring detects misuse, it doesn't prevent it.

85
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:23,000
Enforcement stops abuse. Monitoring only reveals it after the fact.

86
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:27,000
That wraps up this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

